rification.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "CHANGELOG.md", "docs/architecture.md", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/Cordyceps.Tests.csproj", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/SolvePolicyTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/SolveWaitTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/SolverStateTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/TreeSummaryTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/WatchAdoptionTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasSnapshot.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/TreeStatusText.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/GrasshopperContext.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/SolutionWatcher.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/SolveCoordinator.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/SolvePolicy.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/SolveWait.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/SolverState.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolHelpers.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/WatchAdoption.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CommonErrorsGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/DataTreesGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/GettingStartedGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/McpServer.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhDocumentTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.Tree.cs"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-solve-policy.md", ".prawduct/changes/solve-policy.md", ".prawduct/operator-verification.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "warning", "title": "recompute's response contract changes in a breaking way, but the governed_by disposition calls it additive and the CHANGELOG files it under Added with no Breaking marker", "recommendation": "Instance. Before this change, recompute returned success:true, recomputed:true in two cases where it now returns success:false. (1) A document whose solver is off: the old code ran NewSolution(true) and reported success; the new reply is solver_disabled. (2) Any solve longer than the new 30 s default: the old InvokeAndWait waited for the whole solve however long it took; the new reply is over_budget. A caller that passes no new argument and checks `success` after recompute on a long definition will get a failure on upgrade. The plan's norm line says 'the MCP action contract changes additively for recompute ... conforms', and the CHANGELOG puts the entry under '### Added'. This Unreleased section marks every other response-contract change 'Breaking:'. The decision itself is recorded (Decisions made mid-build: 'Every recompute state but completed is success: false'), and the owner ruled the release a major, so the change is allowed. What is wrong is the label. Add a 'Breaking:' line under Changed covering both cases, and correct the plan's boundary-patterns disposition so it no longer calls the recompute change additive.", "files": ["CHANGELOG.md", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhDocumentTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/SolvePolicy.cs"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "SolveRecord.Span and EndedUtc are recorded but never read", "recommendation": "Instance. SolutionWatcher.OnSolutionEnd records each solve's span, with extra logic to drop the span for a disabled document (a verify-api decision), and SolverState stores it and the end time in SolveRecord. Nothing in production reads either. recompute's elapsedMs is deliberately Cordyceps's own wait time, and the only reads of SolveRecord outside SolverState are Generation (SolveWait) and Aborted (GhDocumentTool, outline, tree). A maintainer has to keep the disabled-document span rule correct for a value no reply exposes. Either surface it (for example as `solve.spanMs` on a completed or aborted recompute, documented in ActionInfo and CommonErrorsGuide) or remove Span and EndedUtc and the watcher's span logic.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/SolverState.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/SolutionWatcher.cs"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "No live evidence yet for a user-visible change that rests on host behaviour no test exercises (VRF-023 pending)", "recommendation": "Instance. Every outcome recompute can report depends on three Grasshopper behaviours known only from reading the decompiled assembly: GH_Document.ScheduleSolution(10) actually firing through Instances.DocumentEditor; AbortRequested still being set at SolutionEnd after Escape; and the global Lock Solver suppressing the solution events. If ScheduleSolution's tick is dropped, every recompute reports not_started. If AbortRequested is cleared before SolutionEnd, Escape reads as completed. VRF-023 lists exactly these checks and is still 'pending'. Run VRF-023 steps 1-3 and 5 in live Rhino before this merges, or record an explicit accept that defers live verification to the release gate.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/SolveCoordinator.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/SolutionWatcher.cs"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "Taught workflows still read results straight after solver enable=true, which now returns before the solve", "recommendation": "Scope: class. This change moved what `solver enabled=true` means: it used to solve before replying, and now it expires everything (main canvas), schedules a solve 10 ms out and returns (ActionInfo tip: 'does not wait; call action=recompute when you need the outcome'). The agent-facing workflows built on the old meaning were not updated. Each still shows `solver enabled=true` followed at once by a read, and reads that as the definition's health: GettingStartedGuide.md 'Workflow' block (lines ~36-42, solver on, then `gh_inspect(action='status')`), BestPracticesGuide.md rule 1 together with rule 4 'Check status after changes', Knowledge/Prompts/CreateParametricGeometry.md Step 5 (solver on, status, 'Check for any errors'), and Knowledge/Prompts/PlanDefinition.md step 6 (the same pair). Consequence: an agent following the canonical recipe can read status before the scheduled solve has run, or while it is still running, so it may see expired, unsolved objects or a busy refusal, and it is never told that recompute is the call that waits and reports errors/warnings. The new GettingStartedGuide paragraph at line ~163 states the new behaviour a few lines below a Workflow block that contradicts it. Fix: insert `gh_document(action='recompute')` between enabling the solver and reading results in each of these recipes. Find the members with `git grep -n \"enabled='\\?true\" src/Cordyceps/Knowledge`, and include any recipe that follows `clear` or `revert` with a read. OptimizeCanvas.md already recomputes before status, and CanvasLayoutGuide.md only validates layout, so neither needs a change.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/GettingStartedGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhDocumentTool.cs"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Solve state is keyed by DocumentID while watching is per instance, and the change itself makes two watched documents share an id", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. It broke because revert installs a deserialized copy that can keep the replaced document's DocumentID: the plan's mid-build decision says so, and `WatchAdoption.ShouldForget` exists only because of it. The replaced document stays in the document server and stays watched, because `GH_Canvas.Document =` removes nothing. SolutionWatcher keys watching by reference, but SolverState keys `_solving` (depth) and `_lastSolve` (generation, aborted) by DocumentID, and `SolveCoordinator.FindDocument` also resolves by id. Whenever both documents raise solution events, they feed one record. A start in one and an end in the other unbalance the depth. An end in the replaced document advances the generation and answers a `recompute` ticket taken on the reverted document, so the reply can say `completed`, or `aborted` from the wrong solve, before the reverted document's own solve has run. `outline`/`tree` can show the other document's aborted flag. One way the replaced document keeps solving: `CordycepsComponent.RefreshComponent` (called from `McpServer.RecordCommand` on every MCP call) schedules a solution on the port-owner instance's document. That instance stays in the replaced document unless switching the canvas releases its port. ShouldForget patches the forget path but not begin, end or generation. Fix it at the identity: give the solve record the same identity the watcher uses (a reference-keyed or watch-issued key carried on the ticket), or make revert's document unable to share an id with one that is still live. Then let `CountRuntimeMessages` take the GH_Document captured at scheduling rather than looking one up by id. If you instead show that the replaced document can never raise solution events after a revert, record that in the plan's Decisions, and that also closes this finding.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/SolverState.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/SolutionWatcher.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/WatchAdoption.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/SolveCoordinator.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhDocumentTool.cs"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "recompute's errors/warnings re-implement status's health counting instead of going through the shared rule they promise to match", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `SolveCoordinator.CountRuntimeMessages` counts with its own `switch (active.RuntimeMessageLevel)` over `GetActiveObjects`. `status` counts through `ObjectHealth.IsSubject` plus `CanvasReader.Health` -> `ObjectHealth.Classify`, which its own comment calls 'the one health rule outline shares'. The two agree today only because Classify happens to check Error and Warning before Disconnected and because groups are not IGH_ActiveObject. The recompute ActionInfo tip, CommonErrorsGuide and CHANGELOG all promise that the counts are what `status` reports. A later change to the shared rule (a level demoted, a population filter added) moves `status` and `outline` but not `recompute`, and that drift passes silently. This is the class the learnings rule 'Tracing consumers of a shared helper misses paths that bypass it' names: a path that reproduces the behaviour without the helper. Fix: filter with `ObjectHealth.IsSubject(obj is GH_Group, obj is IGH_ActiveObject)` and count `CanvasReader.Health(active).Status == HealthStatus.Error/Warning`, so all three read one rule.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/SolveCoordinator.cs"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Embedded workflows still verify straight after solver enabled=true, which now returns before the solve runs", "recommendation": "This is a contract-surface consumer-impact gap. boundary-patterns.md's Embedded Documentation Contract says documented workflows 'must match actual tool behavior', and the CLAUDE.md Documentation Audit row 'Prompt templates | Workflow steps changed' applies. Before this change, `gh_document(action='solver', enabled=true)` solved synchronously, so the next step, `gh_inspect(action='status')`, read a finished solve by construction. Now the call schedules the solve (10 ms timer, then BeginInvoke) and replies at once. The canonical workflows still say 'enable, then verify with status': GettingStartedGuide.md lines 37-41 (a file in this diff, outside the edited hunk), Knowledge/Prompts/CreateParametricGeometry.md 'Step 5: Enable Solver and Verify' (lines 42-43), and Knowledge/Prompts/PlanDefinition.md step 6 (lines 92-93). PromptRegistry serves both prompts. Consequence: on a slow definition, an agent following them gets a busy refusal from status. A script consumer (boundary-patterns names scripts as consumers) whose next call lands before the timer tick reads the expired, unsolved document. Its runtime messages are cleared, so status reports a false all-OK. Scope: class. Grep Knowledge/ and Knowledge/Prompts/ for `solver', enabled=true` / `enabled='true'`, `action='clear'` and `action='revert'` followed by any read (status, outputs, tree, outline, geometry). Members are expected outside the diff. A fix needs a way to wait for the solve those workflows end with. Today the only call that waits is recompute, which expires everything and solves again, and it is refused while the scheduled solve runs. So rewording the workflows alone cannot fix them.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/GettingStartedGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Prompts/CreateParametricGeometry.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Prompts/PlanDefinition.md"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "CHANGELOG does not mark the solver/clear/revert scheduling change or recompute's new success:false as Breaking", "recommendation": "This CHANGELOG's own convention puts `Breaking:` on consumer-visible incompatibilities, even changed error texts (lines 20-24). Two changes here are incompatible for an installed consumer, and neither entry carries that marker. (1) `solver` enabled=true, `clear` and `revert` no longer finish the solve before replying. A caller that read results right after them now reads a document that has not solved. (2) `recompute` now returns `success: false` for aborted, over_budget, not_started, solver_disabled, solutions_locked, not_watched and document_closed, where it used to answer `success: true` (an Escape-aborted solve, for example). A script that checks `success` changes behaviour. The change fragment's consumer note states both, so the impact was found. It just does not reach the shipped record that users read. The plan's norm disposition ('the MCP action contract changes additively ... conforms') is the oracle-side form of the same miss. Instance: mark both entries Breaking and say what a caller must now do.", "files": ["CHANGELOG.md"], "fid": "R-8"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 8, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "A solution start whose end is never seen now leaves a document 'solving' for good", "recommendation": "Instance. Before the depth change, any later SolutionEnd cleared a stale 'solving' flag. Now each unmatched BeginSolution raises the depth for good: the next solve goes 1->2->1 and never reaches 0. Until the document closes, recompute is refused as 'already running', status reports solving, and modal inference stays switched off (SolverState.Derive requires !solving), so a real dialog would never be flagged. The plan relies on 'Grasshopper raises one start and one end per solution', and that may hold. But nothing recovers if a start goes unmatched: a SolutionStart handler from another plugin that throws, an exception escaping SolveAllObjects, or Unwatch keeping shared-id state while an adopted document is mid-solve. Consider capping the damage, for example by resetting the depth when a start arrives and the end that would match the open solve can no longer come. Otherwise record this as an accepted risk.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/SolverState.cs"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "timeout is refused when written as a whole-valued decimal, which the int parameters accept", "recommendation": "Instance. timeout is a string parameter, so JsonTypeConverter passes a JSON number through as its raw text. 120 becomes \"120\" and is accepted, but 120.0 becomes \"120.0\", which TryParseTimeout refuses as 'not a whole number'. JsonTypeConverter's int branch accepts whole-valued doubles on purpose ('clients often send 300.0 for 300'), so a client that does this gets a different answer for timeout than for every int parameter. The refusal message is clear, so the cost is one extra round trip. If parity is wanted, accept a whole-valued decimal by its shape (digits, an optional '.', then only zeros) and add an InlineData case for it.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/SolvePolicy.cs"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check: read core.md, tests.md, release.md (learnings-files --for-diff); no rule departed from", "recommendation": "No action. Rules that bore on the diff were honoured. 'A test naming a race/snapshot/ordering contract must actually exercise concurrency': SolveWaitTests drives background waiters against a TaskCompletionSource that never completes. '`int.TryParse` answers \"does this fit in an Int32\"': SolvePolicy.TryParseTimeout decides from the text's shape with invariant culture. 'Code linked into `Cordyceps.Tests` must stay host-free': SolvePolicy, SolveWait and WatchAdoption carry no DebugLog. 'Validate-then-mutate': timeout is refused before any document access, and Schedule refuses before expiring.", "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Backlog Reconciliation: #95 closed by the fragment; related #113 and #114 are open and consistent with the work", "recommendation": "No action. `backlog cache-query affecting` the changed core files returns 0 items. `search solve` finds #95 (closed by .prawduct/changes/solve-policy.md), #113 (revert installs a document the server never sees, which the WatchAdoption decision works around rather than resolves), #114 (an argument the action does not take is ignored, the same rule `timeout` follows for non-recompute actions), and #91/#96/#97, which the plan lists as out of scope. Nothing the diff resolves is still open, and nothing it obsoletes was left unclosed.", "oid": "O-4"}], "duration_seconds": 660, "scope": "solve-policy", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "02"], "chunk": "01", "base_reviewed": "89c3f2c27aa86ca1f9fc8a46ff74beed80423a9f", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-solve-policy.md", ".prawduct/changes/solve-policy.md", ".prawduct/operator-verification.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "CHANGELOG.md", "docs/architecture.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CommonErrorsGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/DataTreesGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/GettingStartedGuide.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01", "02"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifact